Mean reversion remains the base case over the one-month horizon, but with lower conviction. VIX is near cyclical lows, suggesting limited downside, while the expanded vol risk premium argues against imminent compression. The evidence now leans toward vol holding near current levels or grinding marginally higher, rather than material repricing upward. The previous call was too hawkish on direction; the regime looks more likely to consolidate sideways.", "direction": "flat", "conviction": 0.35, "key_evidence": ["vix_level", "vol_risk_premium", "vix_change_4w"], "falsifier": "VIX rallies decisively above 22 or risk premium compresses back below 0.03, suggesting structural vol weakness resumes.", "input_ranking": [{"input": "vix_level", "pull": "down", "weight": 0.4}, {"input": "vol_risk_premium", "pull": "up", "weight": 0.35}, {"input": "vix_change_4w", "pull": "neutral", "weight": 0.25}], "missing_inputs": [{"driver": "risk_appetite", "why": "Market sentiment and tail-risk demand would clarify vol floor sustainability."}, {"driver": "positioning", "why": "Vol positioning extremes would indicate whether current premium can persist."}]}

{"report": "My previous call was directionally wrong. I predicted vix_level would ease (down) with moderate conviction, but the data shows a sharp reversal: VIX rose from 17.45 to 19.32 to 21.71 to 21.27 over the final four observations—a 21% move in the latest period alone. The 4-week change metric confirms this, jumping from 3.61 to 5.53 to 4.51 to 3.75 points, all substantially above the 0–2 point range that dominated earlier periods. The vol_risk_premium has expanded from near-zero levels (0.00–0.05) to 0.08–0.11, a meaningful widening that signals genuine stress rather than complacency.

The FOMC language explicitly mentions "tighter credit conditions" weighing on activity and hiring, acknowledges "elevated inflation," and states the Committee "remains highly attentive to inflation risks"—language consistent with an environment where financial conditions have tightened and uncertainty has risen. The pause in rate hikes (holding at 5.25–5.50%) combined with continued QT and explicit mention of uncertainty around credit effects suggests the Fed sees downside risks crystallizing. This backdrop aligns with the volatility spike: markets are repricing risk as credit stress becomes visible and inflation remains sticky.

